she's somebody with a particular view on  this she lost her 18 year old daughter  ruby to cancer just before this happened  on the 15th of may emma thank you so  much for talking to us today um  what are your feelings today i mean  clearly anger in the house of commons  about this um about the fact that there  appears to have been a party uh for up  to 100 people five days after your  daughter passed away  yes well i think  there have been so many of these now um  and  it's just it's incredibly insulting and  i think it just makes a very horrible  situation  just that bit worse  um and i think what's particularly  infuriating now is that the evidence  is really mounting  and it really feels as though it's time  for the prime minister to just put his  hand up and accept and acknowledge that  what they did was wrong and it was it  was very very wrong and it was very very  insulting to all those people all those  families like ourselves who made  enormous sacrifices and whose end of  life was made that much more painful  because of the restrictions  tell us what the last few days were like  with your daughter  so ruby she didn't die of covid she died  of blood cancer and she'd been treated  for about 10 months most of which was as  an inpatient in hospital  and until three weeks before she died we  thought she was going to get better  but she came home um  from hospital at the end of february and  then was under regular checkups and did  a checkup on the 23rd of april they  found that the council had come back and  by that stage she was out of options  before she got sick she was a perfectly  healthy happy teenager halfway through  her sixth form  and with plans to go out and save the  world so on the 23rd of april she she  found out that the cancer had come back  we knew she was going to die we didn't  know how long it would take as it turns  out it was very quick it was only three  weeks later that she died  we were lucky in that she was at home so  she was able to be with me and her  father and her sister but we were the  only people other than hospice care  staff  who came in to look after her who she  could see during that time so she  couldn't see her friends she couldn't  see her grandparents  they had to say goodbye to her on zoom  [Music]  and we did that because those are the  rules it was a really really strict time  of lockdown then you could only meet one  person outside  and we followed the rules because it was  really important  and we didn't begrudge doing that  because we knew it was it was what  everybody had to do there were no  exceptions  and now it would appear that those rules  were for everybody except for  the government except for number 10  and it's just  so maddening that they weren't following  their own rules it makes me very very  cross  you seem really calm about it i'm not  quite sure i'd be quite as calm as you  are being um  how hard was it for you you've lost your  daughter i mean this is the wrong order  of things isn't it and presumably you  weren't able to be around your parents  to comfort you  yes absolutely i mean  the pain of losing a child is is  indescribable is i think it's the worst  worst pain anyone can go through  and it was painful for so many people it  was extremely painful for my parents and  for her friends and the fact that we  couldn't come together we couldn't come  together to comfort each other just made  it a lot worse it was excruciating  how was the funeral  so we actually chose not to have a  funeral so ruby had been very organized  in her last three weeks um she left us a  list of things she wanted us to do  and she said actually when it came to a  funeral she wasn't bothered by what we  did she said i'm not going to be there i  want you to do whatever works best for  you  and we felt because the rules at the  time were a maximum of 10 people  socially distanced we didn't feel that  would offer any comfort or solace to  anyone i didn't want to put ruby's  sister through that i didn't want to put  my parents through that  so we thought we'd wait until we could  do something later on and then we  quickly realized that was we were going  to be waiting a long time so we ended up  doing three very tiny events when the  rules were relaxed to allow six people  to come together  um but it was it wasn't the big  coming together the big celebration of  life that we'd hoped for and i think  that has made the grieving process a lot  harder for everybody not just for us  and when you think back now to that time  and how incredibly difficult  it was for you  um what would your message to the  government be today  well my message would be  i think you owe us an apology  um it's very very clear that you broke  the rules  it would appear that you don't care that  you broke the rules and i feel very  strongly that we deserve leaders who  will lead by example  we deserve better than this  and it's funny that you said i i seem  very calm about this i i am boiling with  rage inside and if ruby were alive now  she would be absolutely spitting mad  she asked to be remembered by the motto  live kindly live loudly she believed  that being kind  is the most important thing but she also  believed very strongly in calling out  injustice and standing up for what you  believe in which is where the loudly  part of her motto comes from um and she  would not be calm if she was allowed  alive right now she would have made a  protest banner and she'd be banging on  the gates of number 10 and absolutely  furious  so i'm doing this interview for her this  is me living loudly on her behalf  because she would want me to talk up  about this  do you think the government is operating  to to a different set of rules  than the ones that you operated to  absolutely absolutely i think it was  mentioned by somebody just now in the  houses of parliament it's one rule for  them one rule for everybody else and  that's just not acceptable it's not good  enough um it's frankly outrageous and  it's time for them to to confess and and  to apologize  how hard was it for you to stick to the  rules i mean obviously you did um  but there must have been moments when  you just thought i i really want my  parents around me now or  ruby really deserves to have her friends  around her now  yes i mean it was very hard thank  heavens for technology um and actually  the fact that um so ruby should have  been taking her a-levels that summer and  the fact that for her friends you know  it was the first year where all the  exams were cancelled so  had there been exams her friends would  have literally just been starting to set  their a level exams as she died as it  was they weren't sitting exams so they  were available  to talk to her a lot on zoom there were  a lot of online house parties that she  took part in so she was able to be  connected with her friends but it's just  not the same as seeing people in person  um and it was it was very hard what we  did after she died is we actually  created a bit of a  memorial garden in our front garden with  banners and flowers and we invited  people to come past and leave little  notes about what they remembered about  ruby and that became a very beautiful  thing so people came through in their  family groups in pairs under the rules  but there was a constant stream of  people coming through our front garden  which was really the best the best that  we could do the best that we could offer  people under the circumstances  well emma thank you for for talking to  us today and telling us all about ruby  and i'm really sorry for your loss thank  you very much
 

